  <h1>Video marketing for architects</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A twenty-second clip of a finished project can do what your portfolio has quietly
    failed to do for years: make a stranger feel the space and want it. Most firms still
    treat video as a someday project. It is closer to the fastest upgrade you can make to
    the marketing you are probably already paying for.
  </p>

  <p>
    The advice architects get about video is always the same. Start a YouTube channel.
    Post reels. Build an audience. It sounds like progress, and it mostly produces a camera
    roll of half-edited walkthroughs and a subscriber count made up of other architects and
    second-year students. Video is one of the most powerful tools a firm has. Almost nobody
    points it at the job that actually pays.
  </p>

  <div class="answer">
    <p class="answer-label">The short answer</p>
    <p>Video marketing for architects has two jobs, and only one pays this quarter. The first is ad creative: short clips of your work running inside paid social, because the feed is now mostly video, and in our own accounts the people who click a video ad fill in the form more often than the people who click a photo. The second is an owned library of project films on YouTube and your site, which builds trust slowly and is one of the strongest ways to get pulled into AI answers. Homeowners want to feel the space, developers want proof it got delivered. The ad creative is the engine; the library compounds on top.</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    This page is how to use film for both jobs without quitting to become an architecture influencer. It is one piece of the larger system mapped in <a href="/architecture-marketing">architecture marketing</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The feed already turned into video. Your ads should have too</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the shift most firms slept through. In 2025, Reels made up 46 percent of the
    time people spent on Instagram in the US, up from 37 percent the year before, and more
    than half of all Instagram ads ran as Reels, up from 35 percent in 2024, per <a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/01/20/most-of-instagrams-ads-ran-on-reels-in-2025-data-shows.html" rel="nofollow">Sensor Tower data reported by CNBC</a> in January 2026. The place your
    future clients scroll is a video feed now, with the occasional still.
  </p>
  <p>
    So "should we do video" is the wrong question. The feed is already video, the ad slot
    is already video, and a still image now competes at a disadvantage it did not have three
    years ago. The real question is whether the video running in your ads is any good, and
    whether it beats your best photo. In our own accounts that fight is a lot closer than the
    "video always wins" crowd would have you believe (the numbers are in the next section).
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the same paid engine, fed better. The full case for why your buyers really are
    on there, and how the campaigns are built, is in <a href="/meta-ads-for-architects">Meta ads for architects</a>. Video does not replace that engine. It is the fuel that makes it burn hotter.
  </p>

  <h2>Video ads against photo ads in our own accounts</h2>
  <p>
    We tag every ad we build for architecture firms as a picture, a video or a carousel, so we
    can line the formats up properly. Pooling everything is the wrong way to do it, because our
    video ads mostly ran from October on and the photos ran all year. So we only compared a firm
    against itself, in months when it ran both formats with at least $200 behind each.
  </p>
  <p>
    That left 17 firm-months across 12 firms, between April and 25 November 2026, with 42 video
    ads and 73 photo ads. Lead counts come from Meta. Booked calls come from our own calendar,
    matched to the ad each lead clicked.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Same firm, same month</th><th>Video ads</th><th>Photo ads</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Ad spend</td><td>$17,825</td><td>$19,251</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost per 1,000 views (CPM)</td><td>$101</td><td>$92</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Link click rate</td><td>2.22%</td><td>2.54%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Clicks that became a lead</td><td>10.2%</td><td>6.4%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost per lead</td><td>$44.90</td><td>$56.62</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Firm-months where it was the cheaper format</td><td>10 of 17</td><td>7 of 17</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Leads who booked a call (12 firm-months, 7 firms)</td><td>36.5% of 348</td><td>31.9% of 257</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Look at the middle of that table, because it is the part nobody tells you. Video costs more
    to show and fewer people click it. The ones who do click fill in the form about 60 percent
    more often. Our read: somebody who just watched twenty seconds of your project knows exactly
    what they are clicking into, so the curious and the confused mostly scroll past.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now the honest part. The typical firm-month gap was small, video about 5 percent cheaper per
    lead at the median, and photos still won 7 of the 17. Pooled across every month the order
    even flips ($45 a lead for photos, $50 for video), which is exactly why a format comparison
    has to be the same firm in the same weeks. The months are mostly residential work, and we
    did not have enough commercial head-to-heads to split them out.
  </p>
  <p>
    So treat this as what it is: a near tie, with video slightly ahead when the two meet head on
    and its leads a little more likely to book. That is the same advice the <a href="/meta-ads-for-architects#the-creative-that-works-and-the-kind-that-quietly-flops">creative section of Meta ads for architects</a> lands on: start with a strong photo because it is cheap to make, then put a video up against it. For what a lead typically costs across the whole book, the <a href="/architecture-marketing-benchmark-report-2026">architecture marketing benchmark report</a> has the 26-account numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>What video actually does for a firm</h2>
  <p>
    Split it in two and the whole thing gets simpler. Video does one job that pays fast and
    one that pays slow, and firms get into trouble by chasing the slow one first.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Video's job</th><th>What it is</th><th>Where it runs</th><th>When it pays</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>The engine</td><td>Short clips built as paid-ad creative</td><td>Inside your Meta campaigns</td><td>This quarter</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The library</td><td>Project films, walkthroughs, short explainers you keep</td><td>YouTube and your own site</td><td>Months to years</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Most video advice is really about the library, which is why it feels so slow to pay off.
    The library is worth building, but for a reason nobody tells you. 84 percent of US adults
    use YouTube, against 71 percent for Facebook and about half for Instagram, per <a href="https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2025/11/20/americans-social-media-use-2025/" rel="nofollow">Pew Research</a> (5,022 adults surveyed February to June 2025).
  </p>
  <p>
    And in <a href="https://ahrefs.com/blog/ai-brand-visibility-correlations" rel="nofollow">Ahrefs' December 2025 study of 75,000 brands</a>, YouTube mentions had the strongest correlation with whether a brand shows up in AI answers (about 0.74), with backlinks trailing far behind. Ahrefs' explanation is blunt: both Google and OpenAI have trained their models on YouTube transcripts.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read that twice if you care about being the firm ChatGPT names. A project film with a
    real transcript doubles as training data for the systems your future clients now ask
    "who should design this."
  </p>
  <p>
    That makes the library the video cousin of <a href="/seo-for-architects">SEO for architects</a>: the compounding layer that keeps paying long after you post it. It sits on top of the paid engine, the same way written <a href="/content-marketing-for-architects">content marketing for architects</a> does, and never in place of it.
  </p>

  <h2>The videos Google's AI Overviews cite for architects are tiny</h2>
  <p>
    We wanted to know how much of that holds up in practice, so on 15 October 2026 we pulled
    Google's US results for ten questions firm owners type about getting clients and logged
    every source the AI Overview cited. Nine of the ten showed an Overview. YouTube turned up
    in seven of those nine, and 20 of the 74 cited sources (27 percent) were videos.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Question typed into Google</th><th>Sources the AI Overview cited</th><th>Of those, YouTube videos</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>google ads for architects</td><td>12</td><td>5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>how do architects get clients</td><td>9</td><td>4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>lead generation for architects</td><td>9</td><td>4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>content marketing for architects</td><td>7</td><td>3</td></tr>
      <tr><td>facebook ads for architects</td><td>8</td><td>2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>best marketing agency for architects</td><td>6</td><td>1</td></tr>
      <tr><td>email marketing for architects</td><td>9</td><td>1</td></tr>
      <tr><td>architecture firm marketing strategy</td><td>6</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>how much should an architecture firm spend on marketing</td><td>8</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>marketing for architects</td><td colspan="2">No Overview on this pull</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Now look at who got cited. The 17 videos behind those citations had a median of 636 views,
    and 15 of them had fewer than 3,100. The median channel had about 4,900 subscribers. One
    cited clip is a one-minute short with 55 views, and two of the videos cited for an
    English-language question about Google ads are in Portuguese and Italian. That is how thin
    the field is.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the entry ticket looks a lot smaller than an audience. It looks like a video that
    answers the question out loud, in a category where almost nobody has bothered to record one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Google is also listening, close to literally. 12 of the 20 video citations link to a
    specific second of the video, and only one of the 17 videos had chapters written into its
    description. Google is picking the moment from the transcript, so the sentence you say at
    that moment is the thing that gets cited.
  </p>
  <p>
    That changes how you film the library. Say the answer early, in one full sentence, with your
    real number in it. If you do residential, that is how long a custom home or an addition takes
    you from first sketch to move-in. If you do commercial, it is the schedule and permitting
    story a developer is quietly worried about. Then check the captions, because a misheard
    number is a wrong number in the transcript Google reads.
  </p>
  <p>
    One caveat. Overviews move. On one of these queries the Overview showed up on a first pull
    and was gone minutes later, so read the table as one dated snapshot. The written side of the
    same question, which sources AI engines cite when a buyer asks for an architect, is in <a href="/ai-search-for-architects">AI search for architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What to actually film</h2>
  <p>
    Here the two buyers split hard, and the firms that shoot one kind of video for both lose
    half the room. Pick the buyer before you pick up the camera.
  </p>

  <h3>If you do residential work: film the feeling</h3>
  <p>
    A homeowner buys the way the finished house will feel, not the wall section. Video is
    the highest-feeling medium there is, which makes it almost unfair for residential work.
    Shoot the reveal. The slow walk through the door. The before that was a dark 1970s
    warren and the after that is full of light. If the client will do it, the face they make
    when they see it finished sells harder than any line you could write.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cut those into fifteen to thirty seconds and run them as ads to homeowners planning a
    build or a gut renovation, and you are reaching them early, in the emotional register
    where the decision actually gets made. The full playbook for that buyer is in <a href="/marketing-for-residential-architects">marketing for residential architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>If you do commercial work: film the proof</h3>
  <p>
    A developer or business owner is not buying a feeling. They are de-risking a decision
    with a lot of money on it, so film proof. A short case-study walkthrough of a delivered
    building. The owner on camera saying it came in on schedule and leased faster than they
    expected. A time-lapse from empty lot to occupied. That footage answers the only
    question a commercial buyer really has, which is "has this firm done my project before
    and did it go fine."
  </p>
  <p>
    It runs on the same Meta engine, with different creative and different targeting: the
    decision-makers, retargeted patiently through a sales cycle that can run a year. The cold
    read on that whole process is in <a href="/marketing-for-commercial-architects">marketing for commercial architects</a>. A polished case-study film is also the one piece of video worth posting where developers scroll in work mode, which is the narrow, real use of <a href="/linkedin-for-architects#what-linkedin-is-actually-good-at">LinkedIn for architects</a>, a supporting room and never the engine.
  </p>

  <h2>Where video stops working</h2>
  <p>
    Now the ceiling, because this channel has a real one. Video takes time and a quality bar
    that photos forgive. A shaky, badly lit walkthrough can do a firm more harm than no video
    at all, since the work is supposed to signal that you are good at making things look
    right. Good enough is a floor here, not a nicety.
  </p>
  <p>
    And a video is a piece of creative and an asset. It is not a lead channel by itself. You
    cannot decide on a slow month that a YouTube walkthrough you posted last spring should now
    produce three projects. The library sits there compounding on its own clock.
  </p>
  <p>
    The clip, by itself, waits to be shown to someone. What decides whether the right buyer ever sees it, on demand and at the volume you choose, is the paid engine putting it there. That is the difference between a nice film and a pipeline, and the whole of <a href="/lead-generation-for-architects">lead generation for architects</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    So build the library, but do not wait on it. The video that gets you clients this quarter
    is the one running as an ad in front of people who can hire you, plus the occasional
    project film honest enough to double as proof. The one that will not is the six-month
    passion project about "architecture tips," watched mostly by people studying to become
    your competitors.
  </p>

  <h2>A few honest answers</h2>
  <h3>Should architects have a YouTube channel?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes, as a library rather than a show, and the bar is lower than most firms assume. The
    videos Google's AI Overviews cited for architect marketing questions in our October 2026
    pull had a median of 636 views. Treat the channel as a searchable, transcript-carrying
    collection of your best project films, because that is what feeds Google, AI answers, and
    the buyer who wants to see a finished building before they call.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep it second to the ad engine for getting clients this quarter. Chasing a following is
    the version that burns a year and books nobody.
  </p>
  <h3>What kind of videos should an architecture firm make?</h3>
  <p>
    Short and about the work, not about you. Reveals and walkthroughs of finished projects,
    fifteen to thirty seconds for ads and longer for the library. For
    residential, film the feeling. For commercial, film the proof: a delivered building and,
    if you can get it, the owner saying it went well. Skip the talking-head "5 tips" format,
    the internet has enough.
  </p>
  <h3>How long should architecture marketing videos be?</h3>
  <p>
    It depends on the job. Ad creative lives at fifteen to thirty seconds, because it has to
    stop a thumb. Library videos can run much longer: the 17 videos Google's AI Overviews cited
    in our October 2026 pull had a median length of about ten minutes, and 12 of them ran past
    eight minutes.
  </p>
  <p>
    A longer film simply holds more spoken answers for Google to pick from. Give each answer
    its own chapter and say it in the first few seconds of that chapter.
  </p>
  <h3>How much does architectural video production cost?</h3>
  <p>
    It ranges from a phone and a gimbal to a five-figure production, and the search behavior
    shows the appetite: "architectural videography" pulls around 140 US searches a month at
    roughly six dollars a click (Google Ads data, 12-month average to September 2026), which is
    firms actively shopping for it. You do not need
    the top of that range to start. One well-shot reveal per finished project, cut for ads,
    beats a cinematic film you make once and never repeat.
  </p>
  <h3>Do video ads work better than photos on Facebook?</h3>
  <p>
    Slightly, in our accounts, and not for the reason people think. When the same firm ran both
    in the same month, video leads cost $44.90 against $56.62 for photos, and video was the
    cheaper format in 10 of 17 firm-months (April to November 2026). Video actually drew fewer
    clicks per view. It won because 10.2 percent of its clicks became leads, against 6.4 percent
    for photos.
  </p>
  <p>
    The median gap was only about 5 percent, so a strong photo with a direct question is still a
    fine place to start. Run both, and let your own cost per booked call settle it.
  </p>

  <h2>Point the camera at the work</h2>
  <p>
    You already produce the most cinematic thing most people will see all year, and then you
    photograph it once and file it. Video marketing for architects is mostly the decision to
    stop doing that: film the reveal, cut it short, run it as an ad to the buyer who can hire
    you, and keep the best cuts in a library that quietly makes you the firm the algorithms
    already know.
  </p>
  <p>
    Building and running the paid engine that puts those films in front of the right
    homeowners and developers, on your schedule and at the volume you choose, is what we do
    for architecture firms all day.
  </p>
